---
description: Laundry App Backend Code Review Checklist
globs: *.js,*.ts,backend/**/*.js,backend/**/*.ts
alwaysApply: false
---

## Architecture Compliance
- [ ] Code follows layered architecture (controllers, services, middleware, utils)
- [ ] Proper separation of concerns between layers
- [ ] Controllers are thin and delegate to services
- [ ] Business logic is in services, not controllers
- [ ] Database operations use Prisma Client properly
- [ ] No business logic in middleware or utility functions

## API Implementation
- [ ] RESTful API design with proper HTTP methods (GET, POST, PUT, PATCH, DELETE)
- [ ] Correct HTTP status codes returned (200, 201, 400, 401, 403, 404, 500, etc.)
- [ ] Consistent API response format across all endpoints
- [ ] API versioning implemented (`/api/v1/`)
- [ ] Proper use of async/await for asynchronous operations
- [ ] Request validation implemented with Joi or Zod
- [ ] Error handling middleware catches all errors

## Code Quality
- [ ] Code is free from linting errors (ESLint)
- [ ] Code is formatted consistently (Prettier)
- [ ] Functions are focused and concise (< 50 lines preferred)
- [ ] No unused variables or imports
- [ ] Proper use of const and let (no var)
- [ ] Destructuring used appropriately for cleaner code
- [ ] Template literals used for string concatenation
- [ ] Arrow functions used where appropriate
- [ ] Early returns used to avoid deep nesting
- [ ] No magic numbers or strings (use constants)

## TypeScript Specific (if applicable)
- [ ] Proper type annotations for function parameters and return types
- [ ] Interfaces or types defined for complex objects
- [ ] Avoid using `any` type (use `unknown` or specific types)
- [ ] Enum or union types used for fixed values
- [ ] Generics used for reusable type-safe functions
- [ ] Strict mode enabled in tsconfig.json

## Security
- [ ] Authentication implemented with JWT
- [ ] Authorization checks for protected routes
- [ ] Passwords hashed with bcrypt before storage
- [ ] Input validation and sanitization on all endpoints
- [ ] SQL injection prevented (Prisma handles this automatically)
- [ ] XSS prevention measures in place
- [ ] CORS configured appropriately
- [ ] Rate limiting implemented for public endpoints
- [ ] Secrets stored in environment variables, not hardcoded
- [ ] No sensitive information in error messages or logs

## Database (Prisma)
- [ ] Proper use of Prisma Client for queries
- [ ] Transactions used for multi-step operations
- [ ] Appropriate error handling for database operations
- [ ] No N+1 query problems (use `include` or `select` wisely)
- [ ] Indexes defined for frequently queried fields
- [ ] Migrations properly named and tracked
- [ ] Database schema follows naming conventions (snake_case)
- [ ] Foreign key constraints defined with proper cascade rules

## Error Handling
- [ ] Try-catch blocks around all async operations
- [ ] Custom error classes used for different error types
- [ ] Errors passed to error handling middleware (next(error))
- [ ] Appropriate error messages for different scenarios
- [ ] Error logging with correlation IDs
- [ ] Operational errors distinguished from programming errors
- [ ] Prisma errors handled and mapped to appropriate HTTP status codes

## Testing
- [ ] Unit tests for services and business logic
- [ ] Integration tests for API endpoints (Supertest)
- [ ] Tests cover both happy paths and error scenarios
- [ ] Mocks used appropriately for external dependencies
- [ ] Test coverage meets minimum requirements (70%+)
- [ ] Tests are independent and don't rely on execution order
- [ ] Test data cleanup performed after tests
- [ ] Meaningful test descriptions and assertions

## Documentation
- [ ] JSDoc comments for complex functions
- [ ] Swagger/OpenAPI documentation for all endpoints
- [ ] README updated with setup and usage instructions
- [ ] Environment variables documented in .env.example
- [ ] API documentation includes request/response examples
- [ ] Complex business logic explained with comments
- [ ] Database schema documented

## Logging and Observability
- [ ] Structured logging with Winston or Pino
- [ ] Correlation IDs included in all log entries
- [ ] Appropriate log levels used (info, warn, error, debug)
- [ ] No console.log in production code
- [ ] Errors logged with full context (stack trace, request info)
- [ ] Sensitive data excluded from logs
- [ ] Request/response logging middleware implemented

## Performance
- [ ] Database queries optimized (proper use of select/include)
- [ ] Pagination implemented for list endpoints
- [ ] Caching considered for frequently accessed data
- [ ] No blocking operations in request handlers
- [ ] Parallel execution used where appropriate (Promise.all)
- [ ] File uploads handled efficiently (streaming, size limits)
- [ ] Response compression enabled (gzip)

## File Upload (if applicable)
- [ ] Multer middleware configured properly
- [ ] File type validation implemented
- [ ] File size limits enforced
- [ ] Files uploaded to S3 (not stored on server)
- [ ] Temporary files cleaned up after upload
- [ ] Error handling for upload failures

## Multi-tenancy (if applicable)
- [ ] Mart ID or tenant ID included in all relevant queries
- [ ] Users can only access data from their own mart/tenant
- [ ] Cross-tenant data access prevented
- [ ] Tenant isolation verified in tests

## Naming & Style
- [ ] camelCase for variables and functions
- [ ] PascalCase for classes
- [ ] UPPER_SNAKE_CASE for constants
- [ ] kebab-case for file names
- [ ] Descriptive and meaningful names
- [ ] Consistent naming across codebase
- [ ] No abbreviations unless widely understood

## Dependencies
- [ ] Only necessary dependencies included
- [ ] No dependencies with known security vulnerabilities
- [ ] Package versions specified (not using "*" or "latest")
- [ ] Dev dependencies separated from production dependencies
- [ ] package-lock.json or yarn.lock committed to repo

## Environment Configuration
- [ ] Environment variables used for configuration
- [ ] .env.example file provided with all required variables
- [ ] Proper validation for required environment variables on startup
- [ ] Different configurations for dev, staging, production
- [ ] No environment-specific code in application logic

## Git and Version Control
- [ ] Meaningful commit messages
- [ ] Feature branches used (not committing directly to main)
- [ ] .gitignore properly configured
- [ ] No secrets or .env files committed
- [ ] No unnecessary files committed (node_modules, logs, uploads)

## Deployment Readiness
- [ ] Dockerfile present and properly configured
- [ ] Health check endpoint implemented
- [ ] Graceful shutdown handling
- [ ] Process manager configuration (PM2 config if applicable)
- [ ] Environment-specific configuration handled
- [ ] Database migrations can be run in production

## Code Review Specific
- [ ] Code addresses the requirements completely
- [ ] No commented-out code left behind
- [ ] TODOs have associated tickets or are addressed
- [ ] Code follows existing patterns in the codebase
- [ ] Breaking changes documented and communicated
- [ ] Performance impact considered
- [ ] Backward compatibility maintained (if required)

## Final Checks
- [ ] Code runs without errors locally
- [ ] All tests pass
- [ ] No linting errors
- [ ] Build succeeds
- [ ] API documentation updated
- [ ] Postman collection updated (if applicable)
- [ ] Database migrations tested
- [ ] Environment variables documented
